  "query_preview": "..\n\nAn extremely dry airmass is in place over southeast Michigan today, \nas the 12z DTX sounding indicated a dew pt depression of 49 C at 850 \nMB, 47 C at 700 MB, and a PW value of 0.21 inches. The PW value \nappears to set a new daily record low based on the SPC climatology \npage. With this dry air and an upper level ridge expected to hold \nover the northern Great Lakes this weekend, it comes as no surprise \nthat dry weather remains in store. Expect mild days and cool nights \nthis weekend into early next week. Shallow ground fog is not out of \nthe question with the longer nights, but uncertain if the \nsurface/near surface moisture is sufficient. Nam surface \ncondensation pressure deficits stay mainly above 5 MB.  \n\nA slow evolving upper level pattern will feature deep, stacked lows \nnear the Mid-Atlantic coastline into early week. So although heights \nlower modestly Monday/Tuesday over the central Great Lakes, the \nridge axis remains mostly entrenched. \n\nThe next chance of rain arrives on Wednesday as a shortwave trough \ntracks through. Medium range models then significantly diverge, as \nthe strength and interaction between the upper level low/trough over \ncentral Canada and the closed low coming out of the Four Corners \nregion needs to be sorted out.",
